<Base
  title="Become a Resident in Andorra | Official pathways"
  description="A practical introduction to Andorran residency: official permit types, Govern.ad sources, and a directory of named advisors. Not legal advice."
  path="/become-a-resident"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Become a Resident</h1>
    <p>
      Andorra is a sovereign principality. Residence is granted under Andorran
      immigration law, through the Servei d’Immigració. This page is an
      orientation, not advice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Rules, quotas, fees and investment conditions change. Figures circulating
        online are often out of date. Read the current procedure on
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Govern.ad</a
        >
        and instruct qualified Andorran counsel before you act.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>How the official routes are organised</h2>
    <p>
      The Government publishes immigration authorisations in two main groups:
      residence and work, and residence without work. Family reunification,
      study, seasonal work and a few specialised statuses sit alongside them.
      Names and letter codes below follow the Government’s own pages.
    </p>

    <h3>Residence and work</h3>
    <p>
      An authorisation to live in Andorra and work for an Andorran employer
      (A.1) generally requires a contract with a legally constituted company and
      a place within the quota then in force. Self-employed residence and work
      (J.1) is a separate initial authorisation for those who will exercise an
      activity on their own account. Both are described, with document lists, on
      the official immigration pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first authorisation is typically issued for one year, with later
      renewals on a longer cycle. Nationals of states that have a convention
      with Andorra may follow a different renewal path. Confirm this on the
      current A.1 and J.1 pages, not from a brochure.
    </p>

    <h3>Residence without work</h3>
    <p>
      Residence without work (D.1) covers people who will live in Andorra
      without taking local employment. The Government’s page distinguishes
      several situations, including residence without lucrative activity and
      residence for professionals with international projection. A digital-nomad
      authorisation (D.3) and a route for participants in entrepreneur
      programmes (D.4) are listed on the same official residence index.
    </p>
    <p>
      Financial conditions, deposits with the Andorran Financial Authority, and
      annual presence requirements are set in current law and regulation. We do
      not repeat amounts here: they have been revised more than once, and the
      only safe source is the live Government text and your advisor’s reading of
      it.
    </p>

    <h3>Family, study and other statuses</h3>
    <p>
      Family reunification (B.1), study and research (F), temporary seasonal
      authorisations (G.1) and frontier work (C.1) are published on the same
      Government section. Each has its own document list and renewal page.
    </p>

    <h2>What the process usually involves</h2>
    <p>
      Applications are made to the Servei d’Immigració. Official pages describe
      identity documents, legalised or apostilled criminal-record certificates,
      proof of accommodation, civil-status documents, and — for workers — a
      contract. A medical review is part of the published file. After a grant,
      the holder is expected to register with the Comú of the parish of
      residence within the period stated on the official note (three months on
      the current A.1 page).
    </p>
    <p>
      Tax residence is not the same as an immigration permit. The Ministeri de
      Finances and the Departament de Tributs i de Fronteres publish the tax
      rules. Spending a stated number of days in Andorra, or locating your
      centre of interests here, can have tax consequences in Andorra and in the
      country you leave. Do not rely on a headline rate you have heard socially.
    </p>

